A Null-Model Protocol for Statistical Gap Claims in Scale-Dependent Data
A Methodological Proposal
Abstract
Claims that an empirical distribution contains an unusually large gap are common in the physical and natural sciences, where characteristic scales span many orders of magnitude. Such claims are vulnerable to selection effects, measurement uncertainty, clustering and binning choices, boundary effects, post hoc selection of the gap, and the tendency of large samples to contain apparent gaps by chance. A visible gap in a plotted distribution is not, by itself, evidence of structure.

This paper proposes a protocol for evaluating gap claims against an explicitly specified null model. The null density, support rule, scan family, gap-selection rule and nuisance-parameter treatment are pre-specified, and inference is drawn from an empirical null distribution obtained by reproducing the complete analysis pipeline on synthetic data, rather than from an assumed analytic distribution.

The protocol is built around a profile likelihood-ratio scan statistic derived for a one-parameter, one-sided depletion alternative. It equals the two-bin G-test deviance when the window count is at or below its null expectation and is zero otherwise. It is not a general solution to gap detection. For a fully specified continuous null with independent, identically distributed observations, the null distribution of the scan statistic is distribution-free; full-pipeline simulation is needed exactly where the pipeline departs from that case. The central claim of the protocol is falsifiable: if the effective null and the observation model are correctly specified and the pipeline is reproduced in full, the reference distribution should calibrate the test, and a demonstrated failure of calibration under those conditions would refute the protocol.

The paper is a methodological proposal with a preliminary empirical programme. It illustrates the protocol with worked examples and small simulations of calibration and power. These are illustrative, not a validation, and no false-positive or false-negative rates for a full battery of nulls are claimed.

Keywords: scan statistics, null models, gap detection, maximum spacing, likelihood ratio, Monte Carlo simulation, multiple comparisons, selection effects

1. Introduction
Empirical distributions in the physical and natural sciences often span many orders of magnitude (stellar masses, planetary radii, particle lifetimes, binding energies). Gaps, meaning intervals of low or zero occupancy, are visually salient and are often read as evidence of distinct populations, thresholds or organisational levels. Such readings face six well-known problems.

Selection effects. Detection limits, survey design or catalogue rules can suppress observations in some regions more than others.

Measurement uncertainty. Noise can blur or shift observations, so a reported gap may lie within the combined uncertainty of the points that bound it.

Post hoc selection. If the most striking gap is picked after inspecting the data, the chance of finding a gap at least that large somewhere is not the chance of finding one at a pre-specified place.

Clustering and binning. A gap present under one binning or clustering choice may vanish under another.

Finite-sample chance gaps. A smooth continuous distribution produces visible gaps in finite samples. On a fixed support the largest spacing shrinks as n grows (of order log n / n), but the number of candidate gaps grows, and so does the largest spacing measured in units of the mean spacing. Hence "how large a gap is surprising" depends on n.

Calibration. Scan statistics maximise over many windows. Exact or asymptotic calibration exists in special cases, for instance the classical maximum-spacing distribution for a uniform null with a fixed or conditioned support (Feller 1971; Glaz et al. 2001), but it is generally unavailable once nuisance estimation, selection, measurement error or dependence enters the pipeline.

The protocol makes three commitments:

Pre-specification of the null model, support rule, scan family, gap-selection rule and nuisance-parameter treatment before analysis.

Full-pipeline simulation: the entire analysis, including support estimation, selection, transformation, clustering, window selection and nuisance estimation, is reproduced on synthetic data drawn under the null.

Separation of statistical and physical claims. The protocol asks only whether the observed gap is unusual under a specified null. Whether it reflects a physical level, threshold or population needs independent evidence.

The protocol makes a falsifiable claim: given a correctly specified effective null and observation model, and a pipeline reproduced in full, the empirical null distribution of the scan statistic should calibrate the test at nominal level. A demonstrable failure of calibration under those conditions would refute the claim. Section 15 lays out the tests that would establish or falsify the protocol's usefulness, including the failure modes that the paper already documents. Section 13 reports illustrative simulations that begin that programme; they are not a validation, and no false-positive or false-negative rates for a full battery of nulls are claimed.

2. Scope and Non-Claims
The protocol evaluates claims of the form: the observed distribution contains a gap that is unusually large under a specified null model, after accounting for the procedure that selected it.

It does not identify physical levels, thresholds or populations; it does not replace domain knowledge; and it does not decide whether a gap is "real" in any ontological sense. A statistically unusual gap is a distributional feature of unknown physical significance until independent evidence says otherwise. This caveat is stated here once and applies throughout.

The derivation assumes continuous observations in the transformed space, and the effective null that enters P₀ is likewise assumed continuous. For discrete or discretised data (including data on a coarse lattice), or for an effective null with atoms (for example after selection or measurement convolution that places mass on a countable set), the derivation and the distribution-free results below do not directly apply. Where the discreteness arises from rounding or measurement precision, and not from a genuine physical quantisation, the analyst may pre-specify a jittering step that restores continuity; the prescription is given in Section 6.3. Where the discreteness is intrinsic, a discrete analogue needs its own alternative and statistic.

The protocol reports a statistic, an empirical null distribution and an exceedance probability, and stops there.

3. Setup, Support and the Distribution-Free Case
Let x₁ ≤ … ≤ xₙ be the transformed observations in an interval [a, b], typically x = log t for a characteristic scale t. The transformation must be justified by the scientific structure of the problem, not applied mechanically; a uniform null is not invariant to the transformation, so the transformation is part of the null model. Let p₀ be a null density on [a, b] with distribution function P₀. For a window w ⊆ [a, b], write P₀(w) for the integral of p₀ over w, and n_w for the number of observations falling in w.

The interval [a, b] enters every P₀(w) and determines which boundary windows exist. A support rule must be pre-specified:

S1 (fixed support). [a, b] is fixed by the measurement definition, a physical bound or theory, independently of the data.

S2 (data-estimated support). [a, b] is estimated from the data by a stated rule, typically a = min xᵢ and b = max xᵢ, or those values padded by a pre-specified constant.

S3 (externally estimated support). [a, b] comes from an independent dataset or model, with the estimation procedure referenced.

Under S1 the support is a constant. Under S2 the support is random and depends on the same data used to find the gap, so each synthetic dataset must receive its own support by the same rule. Under S3 the support's uncertainty should be propagated or assessed in the sensitivity analysis. Ignoring the support rule is anti-conservative, and the effect can be large. In the worked example of Section 13.1 the correct S2 exceedance probability is 2.99 × 10⁻³; a reference simulation on a fixed support gives 1.37 × 10⁻³ for the same observed gap, a factor of about 2.2 too small, as discussed in Section 13.2. Section 13.4 shows that, at nominal α = 0.05, the mismatch inflates the false-positive rate to about 0.086.

Why a distribution-free case exists, and where it stops. For a single fixed window the two-sided G-test deviance is asymptotically χ²₁. The one-sided statistic Λ(w) developed in Section 4 has a point mass of about one half at zero and an upper tail of about half the χ²₁ tail, so χ²₁ critical values must not be used with it. Under scanning, the situation is worse for two reasons that are standard in the likelihood-ratio literature: the window location is a nuisance parameter that exists only under the alternative (Davies 1977), and θ = 0 lies on the boundary of the parameter space (Chernoff 1954; Self & Liang 1987). For these reasons inference compares Λ_obs with an empirical null distribution as described in Section 8. The formula for Λ(w) is used for computation, not calibration.

Suppose the observations are independent and identically distributed from a fully specified continuous density q on a fixed support (S1), and Λ is computed with P₀ taken from that same q. Then the probability integral transform Q(xᵢ) is uniform, the null probability of any adjacent window is a uniform spacing, and, conditional on n, the null distribution of Λ_max for W_adj does not depend on q at all. This holds for any scan family defined through order-statistic indices, or through a minimum width expressed in units of P₀ probability. It does not hold when g_min is greater than zero and expressed in data units under a non-uniform q, because the set of admissible windows then depends on q. It also does not hold when the effective null has atoms or discontinuities, because continuity is required for the PIT to produce uniform spacings. In a check with n = 30 and 200,000 datasets, comparing a truncated-exponential null with a uniform null on [0, 8], the quantiles of Λ_max agreed to within Monte Carlo error at g_min = 0 (95th percentile 12.71 against 12.70). At g_min = 0.6 the medians diverged (5.93 against 7.79) and the fraction of datasets with empty W differed (3.3% against 1.0%).

Consequently a known selection function or a known measurement kernel does not change the null distribution of Λ_max, provided the effective null that includes them is continuous and is used in P₀. Full-pipeline simulation is needed when this special case fails: the support or null parameters are estimated from the data (and especially when the estimate depends on the selected gap); errors are heteroscedastic so observations are independent but not identically distributed; observations are dependent; or the Λ used does not match the generating process.

4. The Scan Statistic
4.1 The depletion alternative and its profile
For a window w and depth θ in [0, 1], define

p_w(x) = (1 − θ · 1[x ∈ w]) · p₀(x) / (1 − θ · P₀(w)).

Inside w the density is multiplied by (1 − θ)/(1 − θ P₀(w)); outside w it is multiplied by 1/(1 − θ P₀(w)), which is at least one. The depletion therefore displaces probability mass, which is redistributed proportionally over the complement; the outside density is raised, not unchanged. When θ is zero, p_w equals the null; when θ is one, there is a complete gap in w. The window probability under the alternative is q(θ) = (1 − θ) P₀(w) / (1 − θ P₀(w)), which decreases strictly from q(0) = P₀(w) to q(1) = 0.

With ℓ₀ the log-likelihood under the null, the log-likelihood under the alternative is

ℓ_w(θ) = ℓ₀ + n_w log(1 − θ) − n log(1 − θ P₀(w)),

and the likelihood-ratio statistic is Λ(w, θ) = 2[ℓ_w(θ) − ℓ₀]. The profile maximum is obtained through the window probability q rather than by differentiating Λ directly, because Λ is not concave in θ: the term −n log(1 − θ P₀) is convex. Observations inside and outside w are, up to a factor free of θ, Bernoulli draws with inside-probability q, so

ℓ_w(θ) − ℓ₀ = n_w log(q/P₀) + (n − n_w) log((1 − q)/(1 − P₀)).

The right side is concave in q with unconstrained maximum at q̂ = n_w/n. Because q is strictly decreasing in θ, the likelihood is unimodal in θ and the maximum of Λ corresponds to the maximum over the admissible range of q. Three cases follow. If n_w < n P₀(w), the maximum is interior at q = n_w/n, giving θ* = [n P₀(w) − n_w] / [P₀(w)(n − n_w)] ∈ (0, 1]. If n_w = 0, then θ* = 1 and the window is a hard gap. If n_w ≥ n P₀(w), the likelihood is non-increasing over the admissible range, so the constrained maximum is at θ = 0 and Λ(w) = 0; this includes n_w = n.

Writing P for P₀(w),

Λ(w) = 2 [ n_w log( n_w / (nP) ) + (n − n_w) log( (n − n_w) / (n(1 − P)) ) ] when n_w < nP, and Λ(w) = 0 otherwise,

with the convention that 0 log 0 = 0. This is the two-bin G-test deviance in the deficit regime. In the surplus regime the depletion alternative cannot improve on the null and the statistic is zero; this one-sidedness is appropriate for a gap statistic, and analysts who need a two-sided statistic must specify a different alternative. For n_w = 0, Λ(w) = −2n log(1 − P₀(w)). The scan statistic over a pre-specified family W is Λ_max = max over w in W of Λ(w), with Λ_obs the value on the observed data.

4.2 Scope
Λ corresponds to one alternative: a one-parameter, one-sided depletion in a single window, with displaced mass spread proportionally over the rest of the support. It does not accommodate multiple gaps; structured or nonuniform depletion; changes of shape rather than level; extra nuisance parameters; or two-sided alternatives. It is insensitive to regularity (for example near-equal spacings), which a different alternative would target, as Section 13.1 illustrates.

Multiple gaps are a natural case in point. Physical processes can produce several depleted intervals at once, for example when orbital resonances or threshold effects generate a family of characteristic scales rather than a single one. A multi-window alternative, in which the depletion is parametrised over a set of windows and the profile likelihood is optimised over their locations and depths jointly, is a natural extension of the derivation above and can be calibrated by the same full-pipeline simulation. It is not derived here. The single-window statistic should not be expected to have power against a multi-window alternative unless one of the depleted regions dominates in probability mass.

The protocol's contribution is not the specific statistic. Any well-defined statistic — a spacing-based statistic, the Beta-based T_max of Section 7.2.1, or a statistic derived from a different alternative — can be substituted without changing the protocol. What the protocol supplies is the surrounding discipline: pre-specification, a fully reproduced pipeline that includes support estimation, selection, measurement, clustering and nuisance estimation, and empirical calibration of whatever statistic is chosen. The worked examples compare Λ_max against a spacing-based alternative and find no universal winner; the choice among them should be made in advance from the alternatives of scientific interest. The reason to develop the likelihood-ratio statistic in detail is that it makes the deficit regime quantitative, gives an explicit one-parameter alternative, and reduces to the classical spacing test in the uniform-S2 case, which provides an exact check on the simulator.

Relation to established methods. For a uniform null, an S2 support and the adjacent-spacing family W_adj, Λ_max is a monotone function of the largest spacing divided by the sample range, and the protocol reduces to the classical maximum-spacing test (Feller 1971). The protocol adds value only where the pipeline is more complicated than that: non-uniform or estimated nulls, selection, measurement error, dependence, clustering. Related tools include scan statistics (Kulldorff 1997; Glaz et al. 2001) and tests of unimodality or multimodality (Silverman 1981; Hartigan & Hartigan 1985), which ask a different question.

5. Null Model Specification
The null p₀ is the expected distribution without the proposed gap structure. It is a substantive scientific choice and must be justified. Options include a uniform density in the transformed space, for scale-free data over the observed range; a smooth parametric density such as a power law or a log-normal; a mixture model with components that overlap, so no gap is built in; an empirically calibrated density from an independent dataset or physical model; and a kernel density estimate from the data with the gap region excluded. If several nulls are scientifically plausible, report results under each, as discussed in Section 11.1.

5.1 Circularity and the gap-exclusion loop
If p₀ is estimated from the same data used to find the gap, the estimation must exclude the gap region procedurally, and the same procedure must run inside every simulation. The pipeline is: estimate an initial null from all data or a pre-specified subset; select a candidate gap under that null; exclude the selected region; re-estimate the null from the retained data by the same estimator and exclusion rule; and recompute the statistic under the re-estimated null. Two requirements apply to the re-estimation step. The fit should use a truncated likelihood, conditioning on the retained region, and the re-estimated density must then be renormalised over the full support, with the rule for extrapolating mass into the excluded region stated. A parametric form does this automatically; a KDE does not. Excluding the gap by hand and simulating from a null fitted to the retained data does not reproduce the estimation variability and yields a null distribution that is too narrow. Where iteration is needed, the number of passes and the stopping rule must be pre-specified; a single pass is the default.

5.2 Kernel density nulls
A KDE needs no parametric form but brings three issues. First, the bandwidth rule — cross-validation, Silverman's rule, or another — must be pre-specified and reapplied in each synthetic dataset; a bandwidth chosen by eye is not reproducible. Second, with a hole left by exclusion, a kernel estimate leaks density into the gap when the bandwidth is comparable to the gap width; as a heuristic, treat the KDE null as unreliable if h exceeds about g_min/3, report a parametric null as primary, and check the heuristic by simulation for the problem at hand. Third, because the KDE adapts to the data, including large-scale fluctuations that are not gaps, it tends to lower Λ_obs relative to a smooth parametric null; if the pipeline is fully reproduced this costs power, not type-I validity.

5.3 Discretised or rounded data
When the observed values lie on a lattice because of rounding or limited measurement precision, rather than because of a physical quantisation of the underlying process, the analyst may pre-specify a jittering step that restores the continuity the derivation assumes. The jitter scale is set by the measurement precision, not chosen to influence the result: for a grid of spacing δ the jitter is uniform on [−δ/2, δ/2] in the transformed space, or on the grid spacing itself when the measurement is a rounded count. The jitter is applied inside the pipeline, not once at the start, so every synthetic dataset receives an independent draw from the same distribution. The jitter scale must be small relative to g_min, otherwise it changes which windows are admissible; if the measurement precision is comparable to the smallest gap of interest, the discreteness is a genuine limitation and jittering does not remove it. The effect of jittering is reported in the sensitivity analysis. A lattice that reflects a genuine physical quantisation requires different treatment from one that reflects rounding; jittering is appropriate for the latter only, and if the analyst cannot exclude the former, both analyses should be reported. The transformation from the observed scale to the transformed scale must be applied before the jitter is added, since the two do not commute; a fixed grid spacing in the raw scale becomes a variable grid spacing in the log scale, and the jitter should be drawn on the transformed scale with the local grid spacing.

6. The Scan Family
The family W defines which gaps are searched and is a principal determinant of the search. Three families are admissible; the analyst declares which is used.

Adjacent-order-statistic family. W_adj consists of the open intervals (x₍ᵢ₎, x₍ᵢ₊₁₎) for 1 ≤ i < n whose width x₍ᵢ₊₁₎ − x₍ᵢ₎ is at least g_min. Every such window has n_w = 0, so Λ(w) = −2n log(1 − P₀(w)). Under a uniform null this is strictly increasing in width, so the maximiser is the widest spacing. Under a non-uniform null the maximiser is the spacing that spans the most null probability, which need not be the widest in raw units, as Section 13.3 illustrates. Under a uniform null with W_adj, g_min does not change the exceedance probability once Λ_obs corresponds to a gap of at least g_min, because a synthetic exceedance already implies a spacing at least that wide; it matters only through the empty-W point mass of Section 6.4. It matters for results under non-uniform nulls, under W_pair, and for physical interpretability. The multiplicity of the search is not reduced by an observed dataset having only one candidate: the multiplicity is carried by the simulation, because every synthetic dataset scans all of its own spacings.

General pair family. W_pair consists of the open intervals (x₍ᵢ₎, x₍ⱼ₎) for j ≥ i + 2 whose width is at least g_min and whose null probability is at most a pre-specified maximum P_max. The practical scan family is the union of W_adj with W_pair, since W_pair as defined omits the adjacent windows. For j > i + 1 the interior holds j − i − 1 observations, so the general form of Λ applies. The cardinality is of order n², a substantial multiplicity burden, and N must be large enough to resolve the tail. The property that no observations lie inside the window holds for W_adj only.

Endpoint bias and the maximum window width. The endpoints of a window in W_pair are data points and are not counted in n_w, whereas the expected count n P₀(w) treats the window as a fixed interval. The result is a systematic shortfall, specific to W_pair: it does not arise for W_adj, where the interior is empty by construction. Under a uniform null with n = 40 and an S2 support, the mean of n P₀(w) − n_w was 1.03 for windows spanning 1 spacing, 1.13 for 5, 1.50 for 20 and 1.89 for 35. The shortfall matters most for wide windows, where the expected count is large and the observed count is at least about one lower, which makes Λ(w) positive for windows that are not unusual. Scanning all pair windows therefore raises the null critical value of Λ_max — the 95th percentile is 23.8 against 14.0 for W_adj alone at n = 40 — and lowers power. The simulation still calibrates the test correctly, because it reproduces the scan, so this is a loss of power and not of validity.

Two remedies are admissible and must be pre-specified: a maximum window probability P_max, for example 0.5, or a statistic that conditions on the endpoints, as in Section 6.1. A simple continuity-type correction, counting one endpoint inside the window, helped less than the cap, and the Beta-based statistic of Section 6.1 did better than the uncapped likelihood ratio without any cap. Neither remedy dominates the other on the evidence in Section 13.7: at n = 40, θ = 0.8 and w = 0.30, the capped likelihood ratio gives 0.79 and the uncapped T_max gives 0.77, a difference within Monte Carlo error; at θ = 1 and w = 0.15 the capped likelihood ratio gives 0.92 and the uncapped T_max gives 0.65. The uncapped likelihood ratio is the case to avoid: it loses a great deal of power to the shortfall. Between the two admissible remedies, the choice depends on the alternative of interest, and no general recommendation is made here.

6.1 An endpoint-conditioned alternative
Under a uniform null with an S2 support, the n − 1 spacings between order statistics, divided by the sample range, are Dirichlet with all parameters equal to one. The sum of the k spacings spanned by a window (x₍ᵢ₎, x₍ᵢ₊ₖ₎), which is its normalised width P, therefore follows a Beta(k, n − 1 − k) distribution, for 1 ≤ k ≤ n − 2. Define the window tail probability p_w as the probability that a Beta(k, n − 1 − k) variable exceeds P, and the statistic T_max as the maximum over w of −log p_w.

For k = 1 this is a monotone function of the largest spacing, so for W_adj alone T_max is equivalent to Λ_max. For larger k it conditions on the data-defined endpoints and so avoids the shortfall described above. By the probability integral transform the same construction applies to any fully specified continuous null on a fixed support, with P taken as null probability mass and with the Beta parameters adjusted to n + 1 spacings. The statistic is not a likelihood ratio and is not claimed to be optimal; it is calibrated by simulation like Λ_max. It is one-sided by construction: p_w is evaluated only for wide windows, and a window narrower than expected does not lower p_w. Its limitations are therefore the same as those listed for Λ_max in Section 4.2.

6.2 Fixed family
Where theory predicts a gap at a specific location, W may be a small fixed set of windows or a single window declared in advance. The search multiplicity is then small or absent, and inference reduces to a single likelihood-ratio test, calibrated analytically or by simulation.

6.3 Boundary windows and minimum gap width
Two conventions are available for boundary windows. Under B1 the intervals [a, x₍₁₎) and (x₍ₙ₎, b] are excluded; this is the conservative default. Under B2 boundary intervals are treated as ordinary windows. Under S2 without padding, a = x₍₁₎ and b = x₍ₙ₎, so boundary windows have zero width and B2 is meaningful only with padding, or under S1 or S3. Under B2 the null is least constrained near the endpoints and selection effects are often strongest there. The choice must be pre-specified and its effect reported.

The minimum gap width g_min should be the smallest gap that would be physically meaningful, and should be stated as a ratio of scales; for log data it is the log of that ratio. Under a non-uniform null, a g_min in data units makes the scan family depend on the null and removes the distribution-free property of Section 3; a minimum width stated in units of P₀ probability keeps it. Either choice is admissible, but it must be declared.

6.4 Empty family and tie-breaking
If no window meets g_min, Λ_max is undefined. Declare one convention: report "no admissible candidate" with p = 1, or set Λ_max = 0. The second makes such datasets contribute to the left tail of the null distribution. Apply the same convention in every synthetic dataset and report the fraction of synthetic datasets with empty W. When two or more windows achieve the same maximum Λ, select by a deterministic rule: largest Λ; among ties, largest P₀(w); among remaining ties, smallest left endpoint. The rule changes the reported window, not Λ_max or p, provided it is applied in the simulation too.

7. Full-Pipeline Null Simulation and Nuisance Parameters
The core of the protocol is reproducing the whole analysis on synthetic data from the null. Step order matters: anything computed from the data must come after the data exist.

Fix the space. State the space in which p₀, the selection function s and the measurement kernel K are defined, whether raw scale t or transformed scale x. If p₀ is defined on the transformed scale but s or K is defined on the raw scale, convert one into the other explicitly; do not mix spaces.

Generate synthetic observations from the null. State what generates the data under S2: either a plug-in null using the observed range as a parametric bootstrap, or the support treated as a nuisance parameter with a stated distribution. Under S1 use the fixed support; under S3 draw the support from the external procedure. Include any dependence structure.

Apply selection and measurement error, in the order dictated by the pre-specified observation process.

Transform exactly as for the real data, if not already in the transformed space.

Apply jitter if the analysis uses jittering (Section 5.3), with an independent draw for each synthetic dataset.

Cluster if the real analysis clusters, with identical settings.

Assign the support by the pre-specified rule, applied to the synthetic data under S2 or as for step 1 under S1 and S3.

Form the scan family with the same g_min, boundary convention and empty-W convention.

Estimate nuisance parameters and re-fit the null through the gap-exclusion loop of Section 5.1, computing P₀(w) from the effective null.

Select the window by the pre-specified rule, with the same tie-breaking.

Record Λ*_max.

Multiplicity handling across datasets or hypotheses is applied once, after the loop. Repeating for i = 1 to N gives the empirical null distribution Λ_max,1 through Λ_max,N and the exceedance probability

p = (1 + number of i with Λ*_max,i ≥ Λ_obs) / (N + 1),

which includes the observed statistic in the reference set and cannot be zero. The tail definition, whether ≥ or >, must be pre-specified; the default is ≥, and any analysis that uses > instead must declare it, because the two conventions give different Monte Carlo resolutions at the same N.

Nuisance parameters. If p₀ contains unknown parameters ψ, the estimation procedure must be pre-specified and reproduced in every simulation. Options include maximum likelihood on the retained region after gap exclusion, with the exclusion rule reproduced; Bayesian posterior estimation, integrating over ψ in the simulation, which carries parameter uncertainty more fully than a point estimate; cross-validation, when the data can be split and the exclusion rule applied consistently across folds; and bootstrap resampling embedded in the full-pipeline simulation. A separate bootstrap that is not embedded characterises the variability of ψ̂ but does not propagate it into the scan. A point estimate that happens to fit the data well gives a small Λ_obs; integrating over ψ gives a wider effective null. Both are defensible; the choice must be justified and reported.

Reproducing the estimation inside the loop. Let E be the estimation operator. The real analysis is data → E(data) → select gap → exclude gap → E(retained data) → Λ_obs, and each synthetic dataset undergoes the same composition. Any step done by hand on the real data, for example deciding by eye which points to exclude, cannot be reproduced and must be replaced by an algorithmic rule before the analysis begins. If p₀ is estimated from the same data used to find the gap, the fitted null partly tracks the gap, which tends to shrink Λ_obs; the gap-exclusion step is designed to remove this, and it is the step whose omission from the simulation was most consequential in Section 13.5.

Why each step is reproduced. Scanning inflates the distribution of the maximum above that of any single window. Beyond that, an omitted step makes the reference distribution a mismatch for the data-generating process, and the direction and size of the error depend on which step is omitted. The evidence in this paper is: ignoring that the support is data-estimated is anti-conservative; ignoring a plain re-estimation of null parameters was mildly conservative in the one case tested, while ignoring a re-estimation that depends on the selected gap was anti-conservative; a correctly specified effective null makes known selection and identically distributed measurement error nearly irrelevant to the null distribution of Λ_max when g_min = 0; ignoring clustering was severely anti-conservative, with a simulation from the clustered process restoring nominal rates; and omitting a known selection function from the simulation was strongly anti-conservative. No direction should be assumed without checking.

8. Choice of N
For a true tail probability p and estimate p̂ from N simulations, the standard error of p̂ is approximately √(p(1 − p)/N), and for a target relative error r the number of simulations must satisfy N ≳ (1 − p)/(r² p). For a target tail probability of 0.05, the number of simulations required for a 10% relative error is about 1.9 × 10³, and for a 1% relative error about 1.9 × 10⁵. For a target of 0.01, the corresponding numbers are about 1.0 × 10⁴ and about 1.0 × 10⁶. For 10⁻³, they are about 1.0 × 10⁵ and about 1.0 × 10⁷. For 10⁻⁴, they are about 1.0 × 10⁶ and about 1.0 × 10⁸. For 10⁻⁵, they are about 1.0 × 10⁷ and about 1.0 × 10⁹.

A value of N = 10⁴ is a reasonable minimum for exploratory work. The empirical exceedance probability is bounded below by 1/(N + 1); below that resolution, report a bound rather than a point estimate. For N = 10⁴, state "p is less than about 10⁻⁴". Report N, the target tail and the resulting Monte Carlo uncertainty. If the pipeline contains an iterative gap-exclusion loop, the cost per simulation scales accordingly. Where the tail probability of interest is below the resolution of direct Monte Carlo, adaptive or importance-sampling variants of the pipeline may be appropriate; these are discussed in the sequential Monte Carlo literature and are not implemented in the worked examples below.

9. Multiplicity, Selection, Measurement and Dependence
9.1 Multiple comparisons
The primary multiplicity, the search over W, is reproduced automatically by the full-pipeline simulation. Further correction applies when several datasets or hypotheses are tested. When several independent datasets are tested, Bonferroni, Holm (1979) or Benjamini–Hochberg (1995) apply as appropriate. When several plausible nulls are applied to the same dataset, treat this as a composite null: report the p-value under each null and use the least favourable for any single headline claim, or apply Bonferroni across nulls, or define a mixture null. Data cannot be generated under several nulls at once, so simulating all nulls jointly is not available; what can be shared across nulls is a common set of random seeds, which makes the results comparable. When related datasets are tested whose joint null cannot be simulated, permutation-based maximum-statistic procedures apply (Westfall & Young 1993). The procedure must be pre-specified.

9.2 Selection effects
If the selection function s(x), the probability that an observation at x enters the sample, is known, apply it in the simulation by thinning, rejecting each synthetic observation with probability 1 − s(x). The effective null after selection is proportional to p₀(x)·s(x), renormalised; P₀(w) in Λ should be computed from this effective null, not from the unmodified p₀, in both the real and synthetic analyses. The effective null must be continuous for the distribution-free result of Section 3 to apply; if thinning induces atoms, the result fails and full-pipeline simulation must be relied upon without the distribution-free shortcut. The two failure modes differ. If the simulation omits selection, the test is badly anti-conservative. If the simulation includes selection but Λ uses the unmodified p₀, the test stays valid, but it ranks windows by width, so its power is concentrated in sparse, low-completeness regions and is poor in dense ones. The effective null makes the statistic location-neutral in probability units.

State whether n is fixed or random under thinning; thinning makes it random, and conditioning on the observed n is also valid but must be declared and applied identically. State whether selection acts on the true or the measured values; this fixes the order within step 3 of Section 7 and the effective null. If selection acts on true values and measurement follows, the observed density is proportional to (p₀·s) convolved with K; if selection acts on measured values, it is proportional to (p₀ convolved with K)·s. Both are renormalised to integrate to one over the support, and P₀(w) in Λ is computed from the applicable one. Inverse-probability weighting does not preserve the likelihood derivation of Section 4 and should not be used with Λ without a re-derivation. If s(x) is estimated, propagate its uncertainty; if it is unknown, report sensitivity to plausible alternatives. A gap that disappears under reasonable selection functions should not be called robust.

9.3 Measurement uncertainty
If the measurement kernel K is known, simulate it: draw xᵢ* from p₀ and add error drawn from K, which is equivalent to drawing from the convolution p₀ convolved with K. The effective null for P₀(w) is then p₀ convolved with K, after selection if applicable. As in Section 9.2, continuity of the effective null is required for the distribution-free shortcut; a discrete or atomic kernel removes it.

If errors are independent and identically distributed, the blurred sample is simply an iid sample from the convolved density, and with P₀(w) computed from that effective density the null distribution of Λ_max is the distribution-free one of Section 3 when g_min = 0. In a check with a uniform null, S2, n = 40, 200,000 datasets, and Gaussian noise on a unit range, the 95th percentile of Λ_max was 13.96 without noise, 13.95 with noise of standard deviation 0.01, and 13.77 with 0.03. The effect was small and arose at the edges of the support. With heteroscedastic noise, half the points at 0.005 and half at 0.05, the value was 13.85, again close but strictly not exactly distribution-free, because the points are no longer identically distributed. What error does change materially is the signal: blur fills a real gap and lowers the Λ_obs the data can show, so a gap that would be detectable without error can become undetectable with it. Computing P₀(w) from the unconvolved p₀ while the data are blurred is a mismatch; for a uniform p₀ its effect is confined to the edges of the support, but for a non-uniform p₀ it also arises wherever the density changes on the scale of the kernel. Statements about the direction of any error should be checked by simulation for the problem at hand rather than assumed.

9.4 Dependence and clustering
Clustering as a processing step, for example binaries, blends or groups, is an algorithmic transformation, reproduced in step 6 of Section 7. It changes n and the shape of the distribution, and its effect on the null of Λ_max can be substantial. Statistical dependence, whether from a correlated spatial or temporal process, a hierarchical population, or repeated measurements of the same object, means that independent simulation gives a null that is too narrow and anti-conservative p-values, because the effective sample size is smaller than n. Section 13.6 shows the size of the effect for a simple clustered process. Remedies include hierarchical simulation, a fitted parametric correlation model, or block resampling with a pre-specified block structure. If dependence cannot be modelled, report sensitivity to plausible strengths and state that the p-value is conditional on independence.

10. Sensitivity Analysis
A credible gap claim is tested under reasonable changes to the specification. At least vary the support rule; the null model, one for each plausible null; the scan family, g_min together with whether it is in data or probability units, P_max, and the boundary convention. Changing the base of a logarithm only rescales x and leaves Λ unchanged once g_min is restated as a ratio of scales, so the base is not a sensitivity axis; the substantive transformation axis is a different transformation, such as log versus linear, because a uniform null is not transformation-invariant. Also vary the clustering method, the nuisance treatment, the selection function, the measurement kernel, the dependence strength including independence as a lower bound on null variance, the jittering scale where Section 5.3 applies, and N when the tail is near the resolution of the simulation. A result that disappears under minor changes is not robust. A result that persists is more credible, though not thereby physical.

11. Worked Examples
All numbers in this section were computed; code and seeds belong in any publication-quality implementation. The examples illustrate the method and are not scientific claims.

11.1 Uniform null, S2 support, W_adj, exactly solvable
Data. 25 log₁₀ values: 1.0 through 2.4 in steps of 0.1, and 3.5 through 4.4 in steps of 0.1. Specification: S2 support, a = 1.0, b = 4.4, range 3.4; uniform null; W_adj with g_min = 0.3; boundary rule B1; selection by maximum Λ. There are 24 adjacent spacings: 23 of width 0.1 and one of width 1.1, from 2.4 to 3.5. Only the last exceeds g_min, so W_adj contains one window. P₀ = 1.1/3.4 = 0.32353 and n_w = 0, so Λ_obs = −50 · log(1 − 0.32353) = 19.54.

Exact exceedance probability. Under a uniform null with an S2 support, the 24 spacings between the extreme points, divided by the sample range, are Dirichlet with all parameters equal to one, independent of the range. The event Λ*_max ≥ Λ_obs is equivalent to the largest normalised spacing being at least c = 1 − exp(−Λ_obs/50) = 0.32353. By inclusion–exclusion (Feller 1971),

p = Σ_{k ≥ 1} (−1)^(k+1) C(24, k) (1 − kc)₊²³ = 2.99 × 10⁻³.

The k = 1 term is 2.99 × 10⁻³, the k = 2 term is 1.1 × 10⁻⁸ and the k = 3 term is negligible. This formula is exact provided cR* ≥ g_min in the simulated datasets, which requires the synthetic range R* to be at least g_min/c = 0.9275; the probability that R* is below 0.9275 is about 3 × 10⁻⁵ per dataset, so the fraction of datasets violating the condition at N = 10⁴ is negligible. With N = 10⁴ a simulation is expected to give about 30 exceedances, p̂ ≈ 3.0 × 10⁻³, with standard error about 5.5 × 10⁻⁴, a relative error of about 18%, and should agree with the exact value within that error. This is a check on the simulator mentioned in Section 3.

Empty family. In simulations of 25 uniform points on a range of 3.4 with g_min = 0.3, W_adj is empty in about 2.0% of synthetic datasets, and the mean number of spacings of at least 0.3 is about 2.4. Treating the spacings as independent exponentials with mean 3.4/24 would give about 5% and 2.9, overstating both: the spacings are not independent, and the sample range is smaller than 3.4.

Caveats. The observed data lie on an exact 0.1 lattice. The exact p is a correct null probability for the observed maximum spacing, but it does not register that the remaining 23 spacings form an exact lattice, which is itself a strong departure from the null in a direction that Λ_max does not measure. The example is chosen for arithmetic transparency; a publication-quality application would use data free of such lattice structure, apply the jittering prescription of Section 5.3, or report the effect of jittering. The value p ≈ 3 × 10⁻³ shows that the gap is unusual under this null, support rule and scan family; it does not show that the gap is physical. Under a uniform null with W_adj the Λ-maximiser coincides with the widest gap; that is a feature of this special case, not a licence for choosing the largest gap by eye, as Section 11.3 illustrates.

11.2 What goes wrong if the support rule is ignored
This is an error only when the true support rule is S2. If the interval [1.0, 4.4] really were a physically fixed support, S1, the fixed-support value would be the correct one. Suppose the support is data-estimated, but the analyst takes the same observed gap, with c = 0.32353 and n = 25, and calibrates against a simulation on a fixed support of the same length. The exceedance probability is dominated by the leading term of the inclusion–exclusion sum: for 24 interior spacings each with marginal Beta(1, 25) under a fixed support, the leading term is 24 × (1 − c)²⁵ = 24 × 0.67647²⁵ ≈ 1.37 × 10⁻³. This is smaller than the correct S2 value of 2.99 × 10⁻³ by a factor of about 2.2. The mismatch makes the p-value too small.

11.3 A non-uniform null where the Λ-maximiser is not the widest gap
Specification: S1 support [0, 8]; null density an exponential with rate 0.5 truncated to [0, 8], known, with no nuisance parameters; W_adj with g_min = 0; boundary rule B1; n = 30. Data (rounded): 0.040, 0.083, 0.128, 0.173, 0.220, 0.268, 0.317, 0.367, 0.418, 0.471, 0.526, 0.581, 0.639, 0.698, 1.429, 1.556, 1.691, 1.837, 1.993, 2.164, 2.350, 2.555, 2.783, 3.041, 3.338, 3.686, 4.107, 4.642, 5.375, 6.543. This is a construction: points follow null quantiles, except that a gap was opened in the high-density region.

The widest raw gap, from 5.375 to 6.543, has raw width 1.168, null probability P₀ = 0.031, and Λ = 1.87. The Λ-maximiser, from 0.698 to 1.429, has raw width 0.731, null probability P₀ = 0.2198, and Λ = 14.90. The raw-widest gap lies in the sparse tail and is unremarkable under this null; the narrower gap in the dense region is the one that is unusual. A Monte Carlo reference with N = 4 × 10⁵ datasets of 30 draws from the null, scanning all 29 interior spacings under B1, gives p = 0.0167 with Monte Carlo standard error about 2 × 10⁻⁴. The exact value was not computed. Because the null is fully specified and the probability integral transform makes the statistic distribution-free, the calibration does not depend on the null; the Monte Carlo estimate is a check on the code. Selecting the raw-widest gap would have given p ≈ 1.

11.4 A small simulation of calibration and power
Uniform null, S2 support, W_adj, g_min = 0, so the exact p-value of Section 11.1 applies; α = 0.05. These are indicative results from a single run with seed 20261008, not a validation.

For the false-positive rate, with n = 25 and 40,000 null datasets, the full pipeline, with support re-estimated per dataset and exact p, gave a rejection rate of 0.051 at nominal 0.05. The statistic computed on the S2 range but referred to a fixed-support reference gave 0.086. The first confirms calibration for this special case; since the p-values are exact, this is a check on the code, not a discovery. The second shows the anti-conservative effect of ignoring the support rule.

For power, at n = 40, with a window of width w centred at 0.5 on a unit-length support and density depleted by depth θ, using 1,500 datasets per cell, the rejection rates are as follows. For a hard gap, θ = 1.0, the rates are 0.32 at w = 0.10, 0.98 at w = 0.15, 1.00 at w = 0.20 and 1.00 at w = 0.30. For θ = 0.8 they are 0.16, 0.39, 0.42 and 0.57 at the same widths. For θ = 0.5 they are 0.08, 0.09, 0.13 and 0.16. Monte Carlo standard error is at most about 0.013 per cell. The results show a steep dependence on gap width, and little power against partial depletion, because W_adj scores single empty spacings: a window depleted by θ < 1 still contains points, so it seldom produces one spacing nearly as wide as the window, even though the expected count inside is well below its null value.

11.5 Nuisance estimation, the gap-exclusion loop, and dependence
The nuisance-estimation results reported below were computed for three fully specified nulls — an exponential with unknown rate truncated to [0, 5], a normal with unknown mean and standard deviation, and a gamma with unknown shape and scale — under S1, with n = 40 and α = 0.05, using W_adj with g_min = 0 and, in the exclusion variant, a single pass of the gap-exclusion loop of Section 5.1. Each synthetic dataset was tested against a reference that simulates from the fitted parameters but does not repeat the estimation, and against a full-pipeline reference that simulates from the fitted parameters and repeats it. Between 200 and 300 reference datasets were used per test, and the outer datasets ranged from 1,200 to 12,000 per configuration.

Across the three nulls the full-pipeline reference was within about two standard errors of nominal in every case. Ignoring a plain refit was mildly conservative, with pooled rejection rates between 0.041 and 0.043 against a nominal 0.05. Ignoring the gap-dependent refit was clearly anti-conservative, with pooled rejection rates between 0.076 and 0.119, the largest error occurring at the smallest sample size tested (0.113 at n = 20 against 0.083 at n = 40 and 0.076 at n = 100). The omission that mattered was therefore the re-estimation that depends on the selected gap. In runs that also used an oracle reference with the true parameters, the rate was between 0.045 and 0.050, indicating that the bootstrap's residual error is small.

For dependence, observations were generated as 10 parent positions uniform on a unit interval, each with 4 offspring displaced by Gaussian noise of standard deviation σ, giving n = 40. Against the independence reference, the rejection rates at α = 0.05 were 0.765 at σ = 0.03 and 0.361 at σ = 0.06; a hierarchical reference simulating the same clustered process restored the rate to 0.050 and 0.051 respectively. The data here are clustered by construction and contain no gap beyond what clustering produces, so a rejection is a false positive for the question of whether there is a gap beyond what the clustering explains. When the offspring standard deviation was estimated by the pooled within-group standard deviation rather than treated as known, the rates at σ = 0.03 and 0.06 were 0.055 and 0.047, close to the true-parameter values. Estimating the cluster membership when the grouping is unknown has not been tested.

11.6 Pair windows, the endpoint bias, and the endpoint-conditioned statistic
Uniform null, S2 support, n = 40, α = 0.05, g_min = 0, with depletion alternatives as in Section 11.4. Critical values were taken from 30,000 to 40,000 null datasets, and 1,200 to 1,500 datasets per cell were used for power, so the standard error is at most about 0.014. W_all scans every pair window including adjacent ones, other than the one spanning the whole range.

For the likelihood-ratio statistic, the 95th-percentile critical value for W_adj alone is 14.0; for W_all uncapped it is 23.8; with the endpoint-corrected count n_w + 1 it is 15.6; with P_max = 0.5 it is 14.7; with P_max = 0.5 and the endpoint correction it is 10.2. The corresponding hard-gap rates at θ = 1 for widths 0.10, 0.15, 0.20 and 0.30 are, for W_adj, 0.30, 0.98, 1.00 and 1.00; for W_all uncapped, 0.07, 0.15, 0.40 and 1.00; for the endpoint-corrected count, 0.13, 0.23, 0.59 and 1.00; for P_max = 0.5, 0.30, 0.92, 1.00 and 1.00; and for P_max = 0.5 with the endpoint correction, 0.24, 0.74, 1.00 and 1.00. At θ = 0.8 the rates for the same widths are, for W_adj, 0.14, 0.36, 0.43 and 0.58; for W_all uncapped, 0.05, 0.08, 0.13 and 0.32; for the endpoint-corrected count, 0.08, 0.12, 0.22 and 0.53; for P_max = 0.5, 0.17, 0.38, 0.56 and 0.79; and for P_max = 0.5 with the endpoint correction, 0.18, 0.35, 0.54 and 0.82. Uncapped pair windows lose a great deal of power because the endpoint bias inflates the null maximum. A cap on window probability recovers hard-gap power close to W_adj and substantially improves power against partial depletion at larger widths. The caps used here were chosen after seeing that all the alternatives have windows well under P = 0.5, so they favour the cap, and P_max must be pre-specified from the scientific question and not tuned to the data.

For the endpoint-conditioned statistic T_max of Section 6.1, the 95th-percentile critical values are 6.67 for adjacent windows only, 7.97 for all windows with no cap, and 7.50 for all windows with P_max = 0.5. Hard-gap rates at θ = 1 for widths 0.10, 0.15, 0.20 and 0.30 are, respectively, 0.30, 0.97, 1.00 and 1.00; 0.23, 0.65, 1.00 and 1.00; and 0.27, 0.80, 1.00 and 1.00. At θ = 0.8 the corresponding rates are 0.16, 0.37, 0.45 and 0.59; 0.15, 0.30, 0.53 and 0.77; and 0.16, 0.34, 0.56 and 0.81. At θ = 0.5 they are 0.07, 0.10, 0.12 and 0.16; 0.09, 0.12, 0.16 and 0.25; and 0.08, 0.12, 0.16 and 0.26. Without a cap T_max loses far less power than the uncapped likelihood ratio and gains power against weak depletion in wide windows, while still trailing adjacent-only scanning for narrow hard gaps. A capped likelihood ratio performs about as well as capped T_max. No scan family dominates; the choice should be made from the alternatives of scientific interest, in advance.

At other sample sizes and window positions, tested with 1,000 datasets per cell and critical values from 20,000 null datasets, at n = 20 adjacent-only scanning is best or tied in every column and the extra windows cost power; at n = 60 scanning all windows, with or without the cap, matches adjacent-only for hard gaps and is substantially better for partial depletion, with 0.94 against 0.65 for the width-0.3 window at θ = 0.8, and the edge window behaves like the central one. The advantage of scanning larger windows therefore grows with n, and the choice of family should be made in advance with the sample size in mind.

11.7 Selection function
Setup: underlying population uniform on [0, 1]; selection function s(x) = x, so selected observations have density 2x, whose distribution function is x². Support fixed under S1; n = 40 conditioned on; W_adj with g_min = 0; α = 0.05. Three procedures were applied to 4,000 null datasets drawn from the selected density, with standard error about 0.003. Using Λ from the effective null x² with a distribution-free reference gave a rejection rate of 0.048. Using Λ from the unmodified uniform p₀ with a reference simulated with selection gave 0.058. Using Λ from the unmodified uniform p₀ with a reference simulated without selection gave 0.204. Omitting selection from the simulation is strongly anti-conservative. Using the unmodified p₀ inside Λ is acceptable if the simulation includes selection, though the value of 0.058 is at the margin of Monte Carlo error and should be reported as such rather than as a clean pass.

Power against a complete gap was compared for the first two procedures, with 1,500 datasets per cell and standard error at most 0.013, for windows given by centre and width in x with their null probability under the effective density. For centre 0.8 and width 0.10, effective probability 0.16, the effective-null power is 1.00 and the unmodified-p₀ power with selection is 0.03. For centre 0.6 and width 0.10, effective probability 0.12, the values are 0.54 and 0.07. For centre 0.6 and width 0.15, effective probability 0.18, the values are 1.00 and 0.21. For centre 0.3 and width 0.20, effective probability 0.12, the values are 0.39 and 0.85. The unmodified-p₀ statistic ranks windows by width in x and is therefore very sensitive in the sparse, low-completeness region and nearly blind in the dense region, because the selection-induced gaps near x = 0 dominate its null maximum, whose 95th percentile is 18.1 against 13.4 for the effective-null statistic. The effective-null statistic depends only on the probability mass of the window. The two windows with equal effective probability, 0.12, gave different effective-null power, 0.54 and 0.39; this may reflect the low-x window abutting the boundary, where windows touching the support edge are excluded under B1, but that explanation was not tested separately. This is one selection function, one n and one window family.

11.8 Reproducibility note for this section
The example of Section 11.1 is exact and can be recomputed analytically. The examples of Sections 11.2 and 11.3 involve no Monte Carlo for the reported p-values except Section 11.3, which reports a Monte Carlo reference. The simulation results in Sections 11.4 through 11.7 are reported as computed and are reproducible from the code, data and seeds to be deposited with the paper, as required by Section 12. The simulation for Section 11.4 used seed 20261008. Where a single run is reported, the run-to-run ranges are stated in the text. No result in this paper depends on a numerical value that could not in principle be reproduced from the deposited materials.

12. Reporting Standard
Any analysis using this protocol should report the data and the population, and the measurement definition. It should report the measurement uncertainty, in the form of a kernel, or a justification for neglecting it. It should report the selection criteria, in the form of a selection function, or a justification for neglecting it, including whether n is fixed or random and the order of selection and measurement. It should report the dependence structure and any clustering step, including algorithm, settings and how each is reproduced in the simulation. It should report the transformation and its justification, and, where Section 5.3 applies, the jittering scale and its reproduction in the simulation. The simulation results in Section 11 are reproducible from the code and seeds deposited with the paper, and code, data and seeds will be deposited with the paper.

It should report the support rule, whether S1, S2 or S3, its details, and its reproduction in the simulation. It should report the null model, including density, parameters, provenance, and the bandwidth rule if a KDE. It should report the scan family, whether adjacent, pair or fixed, and g_min as a ratio of scales together with whether it is in data or probability units, and P_max, and the boundary convention, and the empty-W convention, and the tie-breaking rule. It should report the gap-selection rule. It should report the nuisance-estimation method, the gap-exclusion loop, the truncated-likelihood treatment, the iteration count, and its reproduction in the simulation.

It should report the statistic, with an explicit statement that it is one-sided, along with N, the target tail probability and the Monte Carlo uncertainty. It should report the multiplicity procedure, including how multiple nulls are handled, and Λ_obs together with its computation. It should report the empirical null distribution, including the point mass at zero and the fraction of empty-W datasets, and the exceedance probability together with the tail definition, whether ≥ or >. It should report the sensitivity analysis and the selection-effect analysis, and the code, data, seeds and software versions. A gap should not be described as physically significant solely because it is visually obvious, has a large score, was identified by a clustering algorithm, or has a small p-value under one null.

13. Validation Programme, Limitations and Verification
The protocol is a proposal. The tests below would establish or falsify its usefulness, and the limitations below state what the paper does not claim.

False-positive control. Generate datasets under known nulls, uniform, log-uniform, power law and mixture, with no gap and apply the protocol. Because the statistic has a point mass at zero when W can be empty, and the Monte Carlo p-value takes finitely many values, the p-values are not exactly uniform; the requirement is that the probability of p ≤ α is at most α for each α, with near-equality away from the point mass. Check explicitly three failure modes: ignoring support estimation under S2, shown anti-conservative for the uniform case in Section 11.4; ignoring re-estimation of null parameters, plain or with gap exclusion, examined for one case in Section 11.5; and ignoring dependence, shown severely anti-conservative for one clustered process in Section 11.5 and untested for other dependence structures. Also check that the jittering prescription of Section 5.3 is reproduced in the simulation when it is used.

Power. Generate datasets with known gap width and depth and report power curves over sample size, width, depth, support rule, null shape and scan family, both adjacent and pair. Section 11.4 and Section 11.6 give single slices at n = 40.

Robustness. Repeat the two tests above under changes of null, scan family, boundary rule, nuisance treatment, jittering scale and dependence.

Comparison with established methods. Compare with scan statistics and change-point methods (Kulldorff 1997; Glaz et al. 2001) and with multimodality tests (Silverman 1981; Hartigan & Hartigan 1985). The aim is to establish comparable performance under equivalent conditions and to identify the source of any difference, whether one- versus two-sided alternative, simulation versus analytic calibration, or support handling.

Empirical application. Apply the protocol to a real dataset selected independently of its development, pre-specified as far as practical, and publish data, code, parameters and seeds.

Limitations. The result depends on the null; no amount of simulation substitutes for a well-justified null. It depends on the support rule; a data-estimated support adds variability that must be reproduced. It assumes the selection function and measurement kernel are known or estimable. It assumes the dependence structure can be specified and simulated; unmodelled dependence makes the p-value anti-conservative by an amount that can be very large and is unknown in general. It assumes continuous data, and a continuous effective null after selection and measurement; the jittering prescription of Section 5.3 restores continuity for rounded or lattice data but is not appropriate when the discreteness is intrinsic, and it introduces a free parameter whose effect must be reported. The statistic corresponds to one-sided depletion in a single window and has little power against partial depletion unless pair windows are added with a cap or with the endpoint-conditioned statistic; it ignores regularity and multiple gaps, and a multi-window alternative is a natural extension not derived here. Full-pipeline simulation is computationally expensive for large datasets or iterative pipelines, and reaching tail probabilities below the resolution of direct Monte Carlo requires either more simulations than are typically feasible or an importance-sampling variant that is not implemented here. Statistical unusualness does not establish physical significance.

Verification status. The protocol is not validated. The externally cited constants and the internally reported arithmetic in Section 11 were checked against their sources and recomputed where possible. The simulation results in Sections 11.4 through 11.7 are reported as computed and are reproducible from the code, data and seeds to be deposited with the paper. The illustrative cases are limited to a uniform null with S2, which is exactly solvable; truncated-exponential, normal and gamma nulls with fitted parameters; one clustered process with known grouping; pair-window power for uniform nulls at n = 20, 40 and 60; and one known selection function. No false-positive or false-negative rates are reported for non-uniform nulls with pair windows, for dependence structures other than known-grouping clusters, for estimated selection functions, for heavy-tailed or mixture nulls, or for real data.

14. Conclusion
The protocol addresses how to evaluate claims of an unusually large gap while accounting for the search, nuisance estimation, support estimation, selection effects, measurement uncertainty and dependence. In its simplest configuration it coincides with the classical maximum-spacing test, which provides an exact check. Its value lies in the discipline imposed on more complicated pipelines: pre-specification, full-pipeline simulation, and separation of statistical from physical claims. Its usefulness will be settled by false-positive control, power against structured alternatives and robustness to specification.

Appendix A. Algorithm
Input. Observed values t₁ through tₙ.

Specify, before analysis. The scientific question and population. Selection effects and measurement uncertainty, including their order. The dependence structure and any clustering step. The transformation, justified. The jittering scale if Section 5.3 applies, with its rule for determining that scale. The support rule, whether S1, S2 or S3, including padding and any external procedure. The null p₀, and the bandwidth rule if a KDE, and the effective null after selection and measurement. The scan family, g_min, boundary convention, empty-W convention, tie-breaking. The gap-selection rule and nuisance-estimation procedure, including the truncated-likelihood exclusion loop and iteration count. The tail definition, whether ≥ or >, with the default being ≥ unless the analyst declares otherwise.

Observed statistic. Transform; jitter if specified; cluster if applicable; assign the support by the rule. Form W. If W is empty, apply the empty-W convention. Estimate nuisance parameters under the initial null; select the candidate window; exclude it and re-estimate; recompute Λ(w) for all w in W under the re-estimated, effective null. Set Λ_obs to the maximum over w of Λ(w).

Simulation. For each i from 1 to N, do the following. Generate synthetic data from the null in the space where p₀, s and K are defined, with dependence; apply selection and measurement in the specified order. Transform if not already transformed; apply jitter with an independent draw from the same distribution if specified; cluster if applicable; assign the support by the same rule, computed from the synthetic data under S2. Form W with the empty-W convention. Run the observed-statistic steps identically, and record Λ*_max,i.

Inference. Apply multiplicity handling if needed, once, after the loop. Compute p as the ratio of one plus the number of i with Λ_max,i at least Λ_obs to N plus one if the tail definition is ≥, or the ratio of one plus the number of i with Λ_max,i strictly greater than Λ_obs to N plus one if the tail definition is >. Perform the sensitivity analysis and report, including Monte Carlo uncertainty and the fraction of empty-W simulations.

Output. Λ_obs; empirical null distribution; p with the pre-specified tail definition stated alongside the value; Monte Carlo uncertainty; sensitivity and selection-effect results; reproducibility materials.

Appendix B. The Gap Score as a Descriptive Summary
The Gap Score is defined as GS = (E_exp − F) / √E_exp, where E_exp is the estimated expected density multiplied by the gap width and F is the observed occupancy. GS is a signed, Poisson-scaled discrepancy: positive for deficits, negative for surpluses. It is useful for exploratory comparison of candidate gaps, but should not be used for inference: it has no calibrated null distribution, it does not account for scanning, and it does not propagate uncertainty in the estimated expected density.

For a single window with null probability P, the two-bin Pearson statistic equals GS²/(1 − P). The likelihood-ratio statistic Λ(w) agrees with the Pearson statistic only when the observed count is close to its expectation, so Λ is approximately GS²/(1 − P) for modest deficits. For a complete gap the observed count is zero, which is far from the expectation nP, and the two measures differ by roughly a factor of two even for small P: Λ = −2n log(1 − P) is approximately 2nP, while GS² is nP. In the example of Section 11.1, GS² = 8.1 against Λ = 19.5. GS values should therefore not be compared with Λ values or read against χ² critical values. GS is not part of the reported statistic or the validation programme. The protocol replaces its inferential role, not its descriptive use.

